\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Discussion}
\title{Following a small quartic through several steps}
\date{2026-11-28}

\p{After one exact integral, a quartic potential can become a
nonpolynomial function, as \ref{fdip-00GQ} illustrates. To follow
its quartic contribution through several integrals, one can pair
that function with a fixed Wick polynomial under a fixed Gaussian
probability. The quadratic part and the remaining function then
have to be controlled at every step. Which bounds on the input
potential make this Gaussian pairing compatible with the
second-order calculation?}
